## Small steps

Depression can make everything feel like a huge effort. Set small, realistic goals, such as getting dressed or going outside for 5 minutes, and notice when you manage them.

## Get active

Regular physical activity, even a short walk, can lift your mood. Your GP may be able to refer you to an exercise scheme.

## Sleep and routine

Try to get up and go to bed at the same times, and keep some structure to your day.

## Stay connected

Talk to people you trust. Support groups, peer support and volunteering can help you feel less alone.

## Alcohol and drugs

Alcohol and drugs can make depression worse, even if they seem to help at first.

## Free tools

NHS **Every Mind Matters** has free tips and a "Mind Plan". **Mind** has information and local services.

## Money worries

Debt and money problems can make depression worse. Free debt advice is available from **StepChange** and **Citizens Advice**.

## When to get urgent help

If you are thinking about harming yourself or ending your life, get help now. Call **999** or go to A&E if you are in danger. Call **NHS 111** (option 2 for mental health in England), or **Samaritans** free on **116 123**, any time.
